\par \pard\plain \s47\rtlch\af20\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1081\hich\af7\loch\sl288\slmult1\ql\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\faauto\fi-210\li345\lin345\sb0\sa51\ltrpar\cf1\f7\fs16\lang1033\kerning1\dbch\af15\langfe1081\sl288\slmult1\fi-210\li345\lin345\ri0\rin0{\loch\lang1025\loch
IMPORTANT}{\loch
\line }{\loch\lang1025\loch
Commands Move.vi and Move (Cluster).vi as well as GetPose.vi and GetPose (Cluster).vi have been replaced by polymorphic VIs, which are called Move.vi and GetPose.vi. When Move or GetPose are selected from the SmarPod commands panel, the polymorphic version is added to the program. The polymorphic VI Move.vi contains both versions of the Move command which can be selected in the context menu under Select Type. Existing user programs that use only the non-cluster Move command will open and continue to work immediately. }{\loch
\line }{\loch\lang1025\loch
(!) Projects that use the cluster version of the Move command will show grayed-out Move nodes. The user program can not be started before (1) the Move command is relinked to the VI (open the context menu on the Move node and click on Relink To SubVI, or (2) the Move commands are replaced by the new polymorphic Move from the SmarPod VI palette (context menu: Replace...). (2) is recommended (calling the cluster and non-cluster Move commands directly is deprecated). Some LabVIEW versions don't select the right version of Move when replacing a grayed-out Move (Cluster).vi node by the new polymorphic VI. In this case the type 'cluster' must be selected manually and the cluster-wire must be reconnected with the VI. The same applies to the GetPose.vi command.}
